WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.402
PAUL YOUNG: So I'm Paul Young.

00:00:07.402 --> 00:00:08.860
For those of you
who don't know me,

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.342
I am the go to market director
for SAP at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.342 --> 00:00:18.312
A bit of history, the challenge,
the SAP world's a small family.

00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:20.520
And sometimes, these things
are like family reunions.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.895
But there are a few faces in
the crowd that I don't know.

00:00:22.895 --> 00:00:26.850
So to give you the history,
I started out my SAP career

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.480
with Hewlett Packard.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.040
I was on board with
HP actually launched

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.600
for a short release of R3
and got sent on assignment

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.160
to [? Waldorf ?] to
help make it run on HP

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.350
9000p [INAUDIBLE] machines.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.350
This is a long time ago.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.460
Release 0.9, if you
go that far back.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.260
And bought it, deployed it,
ran it at HP for eight years.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.970
And then SAP said, hey, why
don't you come work for us?

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:55.610
And 20 years later, I was
still working for SAP.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.860
So the plan was never really
to spend 20 years in SAP.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.610
It's just that's how
it all worked out.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.197
So various different
roles, the last seven years

00:01:03.197 --> 00:01:04.739
of which I was part
of the management

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:06.390
team for HANA [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.185
And in the last 2
and 1/2, I got asked

00:01:08.185 --> 00:01:09.810
to take on a little
side project, which

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.110
was working with the cloud
providers on SAP in cloud.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.210
And the more I
did that, the more

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.960
it became obvious the
world was going to change

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.412
and going to change in
a very significant way.

00:01:20.412 --> 00:01:22.620
And you never want to be on
the wrong side of change.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:27.150
So Google came along a year ago
and asked me to join and take

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.635
on the go to market for SAP.

00:01:28.635 --> 00:01:29.510
And it's fascinating.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.170
Actually, LinkedIn,
on Monday, auto

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.600
generated a message that said,
congratulate Paul on being

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:34.722
one year at Google Cloud.

00:01:34.722 --> 00:01:36.180
So all week, I've
just been getting

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.940
hammered with emails
of people obviously

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:38.940
hitting the congrats button.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:39.440
Congrats.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:39.970
Congrats.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:40.620
It's like what?

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.175
Congrats for
surviving apparently?

00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:44.550
I don't know one year is a
particularly significant thing

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:45.360
to do.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.020
I think more significant is
what we've done in that year.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:50.970
Right?

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:55.230
And so we'll get into the
details and the specifics.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.440
But basically,
nine months ago we

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.360
watched the four
terabyte HANA servers.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.230
That was us significantly
entering this market.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:08.729
And from where we were
then to where we are now,

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.530
there's no comparison.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.850
We are charging forwards.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.590
I'll tell you a separate story
really quickly before we enter

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:22.157
the content, which is Sapphire
last year, which was me,

00:02:22.157 --> 00:02:24.240
I was walking to Sapphire
with one of our officers

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.790
in the CTO team.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.460
And as we were were the
last day of Sapphire,

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.650
we're going out
in the escalator.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.040
And he turned to
me and he said, how

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.680
do you think we're going
to win in this market?

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.590
And I said, what do you mean?

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.560
And he said, well, look,
IBM's been in this market

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:43.867
for 40 years.

00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:45.950
And they've had some these
customers for 40 years.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.150
HP's been in this market,
the SAP market for 28 years.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.900
We've been in this market
for 16 minutes so far.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.880
Like, how are we actually
going to win in this market?

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.280
And my answer to him at
the time was, we're not

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.600
going to win in this market.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.650
Well, first of all, I
was here 28 years ago.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.020
I'm well aware of the history.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.870
And I will tell you
right now, we're not

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.300
going to win in this market.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.800
What we're going to do is we're
going to change this market.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.592
And we're going to make
everyone else compete with us.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:15.040
To which, afterwards, long
time afterwards he said,

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.070
I looked at you, and I
thought, is he insane?

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.370
To the point where I said that.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.040
I'm not insane.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.920
This is a market
that needs to change.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.270
This is a market where
the customers want things

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.630
that they've never got.

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.850
They didn't get from
their original providers.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.230
They didn't get from
the cloud providers.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.680
And we're going to deliver
it to them as part of Google.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.682
So just take that
context in mind

00:03:34.682 --> 00:03:36.640
as we go through the rest
of this conversation.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:37.480
All right?

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:44.320
So first up, imagine many of
you are customers, but imagine

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.230
you are a customer.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:49.717
And for those of you, this is
theater of the mind, people.

00:03:49.717 --> 00:03:51.550
For those of you who
are customers, you are.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.425
For the rest of you who
are Google employees,

00:03:53.425 --> 00:03:55.540
partners, everything
else, just put yourself

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.490
in the mindset of
an SAP customer.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:58.570
All right?

00:03:58.570 --> 00:03:59.240
And it's funny.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.510
When I do this internally,
I always say, this is not

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:02.990
intended to be egocentric.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.100
But a lot of them
look like me, frankly.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:05.710
Right?

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.252
They've been doing
this for 30 years.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:14.070
The bottom line in all of
this, they're fundamentally

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.810
paid not to take risks.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.040
Right?

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.390
SAP runs their business.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.620
If SAP is down, their
business doesn't run.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.453
We'll talk about this
a little bit later on.

00:04:25.453 --> 00:04:26.450
But I mean, it's funny.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.080
We wrote a slide
a little while ago

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.610
on just minimizing downtime.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:33.340
And we had a life science
customer who came to us,

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.860
and the team was debating the
cost of the infrastructure,

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:36.750
the cost of everything else.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.185
And as we went through it, the
CIO said, wait, wait, wait,

00:04:39.185 --> 00:04:39.745
wait.

00:04:39.745 --> 00:04:41.370
I heard some stuff
in this conversation

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.540
about how you could
minimize downtime.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.050
He said well, look, and
if you're in life sciences

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.410
right now, either pharmaceutical
or in medical device, what

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:53.280
happens is,
fundamentally, the way

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.490
the law is written at
this time that if SAP

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.750
isn't up and running,
you can't manufacture.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:04:59.250
Right?

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.750
If you're in
pharmaceuticals, you're

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.180
required to identify for
every pill you manufacture

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.222
all the source ingredients,
where they came from,

00:05:05.222 --> 00:05:07.290
which batches they came
from, the recipe that

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.000
was used to make them, the
production equipment that

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.020
was made to use them, right?

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.330
If SAP isn't online, you can't
record any of that stuff.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.038
Everything stops.

00:05:13.038 --> 00:05:15.960
If you're in the medical device,
if you're making pacemakers,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.727
you're required to identify
all the components,

00:05:18.727 --> 00:05:19.935
all the wires, the batteries.

00:05:19.935 --> 00:05:21.615
So if something
goes wrong, the FDA

00:05:21.615 --> 00:05:23.365
literally you have to
be able to, in fact,

00:05:23.365 --> 00:05:25.032
in under an hour come
back and tell them

00:05:25.032 --> 00:05:27.720
every patient in the planet
that has that battery embedded

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:28.700
inside their body.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:29.760
Right?

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.100
If SAP isn't online,
the world stops.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:33.600
Right?

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.020
Downtime is a huge thing.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.320
And his example was,
we're debating cost.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.640
We're doing everything else.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.090
No one ever comes to me and
talks about lowering my cost

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.600
and increasing my uptime.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:44.100
Right?

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.540
They tell me how I can spend
a lot more to get uptime.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.220
But they don't tell me how I
can do both at the same time.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:52.750
It's like, just to give you
a perspective, in their case,

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.500
they're a $10 billion
a year manufacturer.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.240
His answer is, if
SAP is down, we

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:57.990
have to stop all the
manufacturing plants.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.020
And it costs us a
million dollars an hour

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.520
to shut down the
manufacturing plants.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.300
And we've told that story
a number of times since.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.650
And what's becoming
hilarious about this

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:12.780
is the need for other customers
to then one up that story.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.890
And so I told that
story recently,

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.140
and a customer came and said,
it's $10 million an hour

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.890
for us, or $10 million
an hour when SAP is down.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:20.740
Right?

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:22.430
And so I get it.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:22.930
Right?

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:24.520
I get the challenge.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.470
I've run these systems myself.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:30.532
Ultimately, the objective is
to keep it running because it's

00:06:30.532 --> 00:06:31.490
what runs the business.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:32.540
And if the business
isn't running,

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:33.280
you're not making money.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:34.720
And a lot of your
friends and colleagues

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.428
and stock investments
and everything else

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:37.820
are dependent on that.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.210
And so the point where you
say, let's move to the cloud,

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:41.755
you can perceive that as risk.

00:06:41.755 --> 00:06:43.630
Candidly, the point
where you say, hey, let's

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:44.870
move to Google Cloud.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:45.370
All right?

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:48.880
They're the guys that have been
in this business 60 minutes.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:50.170
Is that really a risk?

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:50.950
All right?

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:52.430
And how do we handle that?

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:53.390
And so it's not.

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:53.890
Right?

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:55.660
We have a very talented
group of people

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.540
who know a lot about
SAP who've come here.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.290
We're doing a lot in
engineering that I'll

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:03.040
update you on in order to
make that a very safe bet.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.208
But equally, I've said
this a couple of times

00:07:05.208 --> 00:07:07.000
throughout presentations
we've made so far,

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.500
I'm also brutally aware that the
number one sales force for SAP

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.050
is SAP's own customers, right?

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.775
Generally, SAP's customers
buy from SAP's customers.

00:07:16.775 --> 00:07:17.900
One of them does something.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.250
They prove it works.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.030
And then they go tell
all their friends.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.185
And they carry through.

00:07:22.185 --> 00:07:23.560
And so in that
context, I'll also

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.060
just pause at the
start of this here

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.880
to say I'm incredibly grateful
for the customers who've

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.780
who come to us over the course
of the past nine months.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.610
And we made a very deliberate
strategy of going to market

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:38.380
and saying, look, we can start
small and get small customers

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.700
and try to build over the
course of many, many years.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:42.010
But the reality of
this market, again,

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:43.540
is you start small, you
get a small customer.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.380
You go to the next one,
they say, well, yeah,

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:46.422
but I'm bigger than them.

00:07:46.422 --> 00:07:47.378
Will it work for me?

00:07:47.378 --> 00:07:48.670
And so we do it the other side.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:51.332
You're saying, who are the
biggest guys that we can get?

00:07:51.332 --> 00:07:53.040
Who are the biggest
customers we can get,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.060
so no one will question that
we're serious about enterprise.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.500
No one will question this works.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.300
No one will question
we can do scale.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.920
And, boy, did the
customers step up.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.670
So I've already been
on stage with McKesson.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:07.240
Obviously, McKesson
is Fortune 6.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.490
Literally, health care doesn't
work if McKesson isn't working.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:12.070
Within the past
week, we've announced

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:14.650
that we have their
contract for their SAP

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.362
environment among other things.

00:08:17.362 --> 00:08:19.098
We've had-- who
else have we had?

00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:20.140
We've had Metro on stage.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:23.545
They're formerly the number
two retailer in Europe.

00:08:23.545 --> 00:08:25.420
Home Depot has done
speeches for us recently.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:29.110
They're number three
retailer in the US.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.900
That's just a small
snapshot of the customers.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.260
You see a lot more names
come out over the period

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.870
here for the big
huge customers that

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.190
have come to Google
Cloud along the way.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:43.220
And so that's an incredible
foundation to go from.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.020
And hopefully, we've
set the foundation

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.620
in place for everyone to adopt.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:51.450
That said, that does come back
to, yeah, yeah, yeah, all great

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.450
there, Mr. Talking Head.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.970
What are you going to do for me?

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:56.670
So let's talk about you.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.640
So three key
fundamentals that we're

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.100
trying to get to here in
SAP on Google Cloud, number

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.400
one of which is
simplified deployments.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.670
Two words, a lot of
complexity behind that, right?

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.810
But I mean, we'll go
through some of the details.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:13.590
But what I'll tell you right
now is for almost everybody

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.392
else in this business in the
large scale environments,

00:09:16.392 --> 00:09:17.850
so anything above
4 terabytes, it's

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.850
a 12 week lead time to deliver
a server to you right now

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.380
minimum.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.660
For our entire fleet, globally,
all of our SAP equipment

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:29.950
is 30 second provisioning.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.670
30 seconds after
you click console,

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:37.200
you have an SAP server really
ready to go at any scale.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.127
All right?

00:09:39.127 --> 00:09:40.710
There's a simplification
part to that,

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:44.340
but there's also it just
unlocks potential in a huge way.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:44.840
Right?

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.340
So instead of saying, hey, I
have a really great idea, boss.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.320
And them saying, yeah,
put a proposal together.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.480
Maybe we'll have some
spare time a year from now

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:52.850
when we've got a server
that's not in use,

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:54.060
and you can play with it.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:55.950
You can build a
server in 30 seconds.

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:58.620
Then if it makes sense and it
does good for your business,

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.750
you can roll forward from there.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:02.427
No one else is close to that.

00:10:02.427 --> 00:10:03.760
Reduced risk becomes a big deal.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:04.300
We've talk about some of this.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:05.633
But I won't go into the details.

00:10:05.633 --> 00:10:08.570
But again, downtime
matters in a huge way here.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:09.090
Right?

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:12.180
Availability matters
in a huge way.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.090
Something that we set out
to engineer into our SAP

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:16.270
environments from the start.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:16.770
Right?

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:19.080
This wasn't let's
just build some boxes

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:20.660
and let the customer
manage this.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.410
There is a clear
recognition from our side

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.000
that, historically, high
availability disaster recovery

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.070
are a really big
deal for customers,

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:28.890
especially DR is
really hard to do.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.118
You tend to end up in
third party [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:31.118 --> 00:10:33.660
you're never entirely sure if
it works or if it doesn't work.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.190
Huge amount of
work from our side

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:36.905
to automate the
deployment of that,

00:10:36.905 --> 00:10:38.280
to make it as
secure as possible,

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:39.738
to make it as
reliable as possible.

00:10:39.738 --> 00:10:42.828
So DR happens just by
default, all right?

00:10:42.828 --> 00:10:44.370
And you don't have
to spend your time

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.140
worrying about the
back end infrastructure

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:48.348
and paying, no thanks to
the consultants in the room,

00:10:48.348 --> 00:10:51.540
but paying consultants
to wire all that up.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.090
It just comes out of
the box and works.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:54.747
All right?

00:10:54.747 --> 00:10:56.580
And then intelligence
becomes the last part.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.695
I think this is the
other component where

00:10:58.695 --> 00:11:00.570
I think the really
fascinating part of Google

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.990
as a company and Google Cloud,
and Google Cloud, I should say,

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:07.620
and the bigger Google in what
we can do for you is this

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:10.195
isn't just, hey, we do boxes.

00:11:10.195 --> 00:11:12.150
All right?

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:14.220
The bigger Google has a
huge amount of assets.

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.200
When you get into
the work we're doing

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.160
with BigQuery and the
relationship to BigQuery.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.630
When you look at the
data sets that we

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.380
have, so the cloud for
marketing data set,

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:22.430
where if you're a retailer
or a consumer packaged

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:26.690
goods company or an airline or
anyone else, the data we have,

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:28.315
it's anonymized,
but tells you what's

00:11:28.315 --> 00:11:30.190
going on in your
advertising, what's going on

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:31.148
in your target markets.

00:11:31.148 --> 00:11:33.960
The ability to join that all
the way through and get a better

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:37.190
algorithm and better decision
making and better results

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.030
puts us apart from the
competition, right?

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.230
This is not just, how
many boxes do you want?

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:43.980
I think, frankly, the
McKesson guys put it

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:45.952
best, which is, we
just don't view this

00:11:45.952 --> 00:11:47.160
as being a move to the cloud.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:50.553
We view this as a future
architecture for our business.

00:11:50.553 --> 00:11:52.470
I'm not sure you say
that about the other guys

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:52.960
in this business.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:53.460
Right?

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.550
But that's where we are.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.530
So with that said, let's get
