00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:02.050
If you want a 12 terabyte
server from any other providers

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:04.960
right now, you have
to place an order.

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:06.240
It typically takes 12 weeks.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:09.545
And you have to pay up
front for the server,

00:18:09.545 --> 00:18:11.170
because it's hosting
by any other name.

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:11.620
It's not cloud.

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:12.400
It's not elastic.

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:15.040
You are paying them
for the server,

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:17.500
for them to buy it and
deploy it and then rent it

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:22.180
to you on a fixed three
or five year contract.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:24.040
It's hardware
running in the cloud.

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:24.740
It's hosting.

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:26.630
It's not cloud as
I understand it.

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:30.070
So here is my understanding
having done this job.

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:33.430
Most of you don't actually
need that capacity right now.

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:34.270
Right?

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:39.115
So for the history of time,
the way it works at SAP

00:18:39.115 --> 00:18:40.990
is when you want to
deploy a new environment,

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:44.170
you go to SAP Quick Sizer,
and you put in all the details

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:48.370
of what you think will be the
busiest hour in the next three

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:50.740
years with the most
number of users

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:52.240
and the most number
of transactions.

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:55.660
And Quick Sizer comes back
and says, to handle that hour,

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:58.580
you will require
this much hardware.

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:01.090
Then you go buy that much
hardware and you plug it in.

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:02.470
Technically, you don't
buy that much hardware.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:04.387
What you then do is you
go, what if I'm wrong?

00:19:04.387 --> 00:19:05.110
So you add 10%.

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:07.360
And the next up line goes,
well, what if he was wrong?

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:08.027
And you add 10%.

00:19:08.027 --> 00:19:10.990
And you buy way more hardware
that you need in the hope

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:12.762
that, three years from
now, you're right.

00:19:12.762 --> 00:19:15.220
And I have a question I've used
in a lot of these speeches.

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:17.500
So if you've heard it
before, please don't cheat.

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:20.390
But I was actually
at the CX forum

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:22.750
for all SAP's conference
for us retailers.

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:25.070
And from the stage I
said, well, two things,

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:27.070
number one of which, does
anyone in the audience

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:31.430
know why Quick Sizer works
on a three year timeline?

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:34.420
Does anyone in the audience
know why SAP picked three years?

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:36.170
I like the puzzled
looks I'm getting here.

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:39.180
It's a question no
one ever thought of.

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:42.005
I'll tell you why, because,
once again, I was there.

00:19:42.005 --> 00:19:43.380
Quick Sizer runs
over three years

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:46.755
because hardware depreciates
over three years.

00:19:46.755 --> 00:19:48.880
And the plan was build
Quick Sizer for three years,

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.797
so when we get to year
three, and you discover

00:19:50.797 --> 00:19:53.220
it's too small, it'll
already have depreciated

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:55.658
and so no one is going to
complain about the cost.

00:19:55.658 --> 00:19:57.450
There's no business
reason for three years.

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:59.283
There's no operation
reason for three years.

00:19:59.283 --> 00:20:01.150
It was driven by
hardware depreciation.

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:03.900
But every SAP customer
has done it that way

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:05.850
from the beginning of time.

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:07.100
Why?

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:07.910
Right?

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:10.035
And the second question I
asked in the retail store

00:20:10.035 --> 00:20:12.580
was, this was late 2018,
so my question was,

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:14.580
so if we've got to go
three years in the future,

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:16.070
let's go three years back.

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:19.670
Anyone in the audience
