00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:25.980
No?

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:27.060
I'll help you.

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:31.080
It was an Elsa doll,
because "Frozen" was out.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:34.140
And the fact that none of us
knows that means we barely

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:35.880
know anything about history.

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:39.930
So anyone who wants to
tell me what the size

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:44.233
they need in 2022 for their
business, please step up.

00:20:44.233 --> 00:20:45.900
Even better, anyone
in the audience that

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:48.752
knows the best
selling toy in 2022,

00:20:48.752 --> 00:20:51.210
please come talk to me because
we're in the wrong business.

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:51.710
Right?

00:20:54.550 --> 00:20:57.500
We should know by now, the
future is not predictable.

00:20:57.500 --> 00:20:58.403
Right?

00:20:58.403 --> 00:21:00.820
We have no idea what's going
to happen with our companies.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:02.080
We have no idea
how fast they grow.

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:03.360
We have no idea
who we merge with.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:04.485
We have no idea who we buy.

00:21:04.485 --> 00:21:06.282
But we go and we
do a Quick Sizer

00:21:06.282 --> 00:21:07.990
and we take our best
guess at where we're

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:09.430
going to be for three years.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:12.040
And then we try and fit
the business back to that.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:14.380
My question always is, so
you go, you make your plan.

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:15.220
You're done.

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:16.370
You buy all your hardware.

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:16.870
It sets up.

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:18.520
You're setting your contract
with a cloud provider.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:20.228
And the CEO walks in
the office and goes,

00:21:20.228 --> 00:21:21.550
we're going to do a merger.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.210
What do you do then?

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:23.710
Right?

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:25.510
And the answer is
write all that cost off

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:27.580
in project cost for the merger.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.170
It's not efficient.

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:30.100
So let's roll back.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:32.275
Let's say I don't
know how big you're

00:21:32.275 --> 00:21:33.400
going to be in three years.

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:36.067
Let's say you don't know how big
you're going to be three years.

00:21:36.067 --> 00:21:42.010
Let's say we don't care
because we don't care.

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:45.640
My answer when customers
ask me about this is, look,

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:48.450
I only really care about
two questions on this slide.

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:53.050
Number one of which is, tell
me how big you are right now,

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:54.850
and tell me you
don't honestly think

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:56.600
that in the next three
years you'll go off

00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:58.450
the top end of that chart.

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:01.200
As long as those two things
are true, we have a deal.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:02.257
All right?

00:22:02.257 --> 00:22:04.590
So let's imagine that right
now, you're rolling out S/4.

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:05.280
You're growing.

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:06.150
You're doing consolidations.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:07.370
You're doing everything else.

00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:09.370
And you go to the other
guys, and the other guys

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:12.567
say, hey, you know, you need 12
terabytes three years from now.

00:22:12.567 --> 00:22:14.400
So I need you to sign
a three year contract,

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.380
take a 12 terabyte
system, pay it up front,

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.420
and you'll be good.

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:17.975
Right?

00:22:17.975 --> 00:22:18.850
That's the left side.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:20.308
Let's say you come
to Google Cloud,

00:22:20.308 --> 00:22:22.350
and you say, hey, I
need 4 terabytes now.

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:26.910
I need eight in next year.

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:30.810
And I need 12 in the third year.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:32.320
I could do that.

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:34.860
All I need you to do is say,
this is what I need now.

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:38.100
And then as you grow, you just
grow the consumption on the VM.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:42.000
You just resize the VM upwards
as you go, and it happens.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.130
And you just pay me
for what you're using,

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:46.870
not what you'll need in
three years from now, right?

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:50.115
I've taken the risk
out of this equation.

00:22:50.115 --> 00:22:52.490
All you need to do is pay me
for what you need right now.

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:55.070
That dark blue box, by
the way, is the difference

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:56.420
in price for a 12 terabyte.

00:22:56.420 --> 00:22:58.912
If you take a 12 terabyte
commit from the other guys,

00:22:58.912 --> 00:23:01.120
or you come to me and you
step up a four and an eight

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:04.100
and a 12 as you need it, the
savings, that dark blue box,

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:09.940
is $550,000 per server at least.

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:13.360
You need four of
them to start with.

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:15.285
This is not a small
amount of money.

00:23:15.285 --> 00:23:16.910
And it applies across
the entire fleet.

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:18.080
There is a retailer.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:20.080
I won't name them, but
there is a large retailer

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:24.248
we're working with who is
doing exactly this right now.

00:23:24.248 --> 00:23:25.790
Instead of doing a
three year sizing,

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:28.290
instead of doing a year sizing,
what they've basically done

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:31.790
is size June, July, and they've
committed to the June, July

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:33.140
capacity they need.

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:35.432
And for most of the year,
they run June, July capacity.

00:23:35.432 --> 00:23:36.890
And then October,
they come through

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:39.133
and say, what does Christmas
look like this year?

00:23:39.133 --> 00:23:40.550
And they come and
effectively spot

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:42.500
buy capacity from
us for October,

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:45.535
sorry, for November, December,
and the first half of January.

00:23:45.535 --> 00:23:46.910
And then they give
it back to us,

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:48.952
and they resize back to
what they think next year

00:23:48.952 --> 00:23:50.950
looks like in capacity.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:54.000
Do the math in your heads for
how much money that saves you.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:55.110
Right?

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:58.080
And so I'll go back to
that earlier statement.

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:00.240
I left Sapphire last
year with a statement,

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:04.350
which is, we're not going to try
and compete in this business.

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:06.330
We're going to
change this business.

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:08.610
And we're going to make
everyone else compete with us.

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:09.750
Right?

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:11.368
And it's been a long, hard run.

00:24:11.368 --> 00:24:13.660
And I'm very appreciative
for everything that was done.

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.810
But as that six and
that 12 comes online,

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:18.183
as the 18 comes online
in the end of the year,

00:24:18.183 --> 00:24:19.350
the world's going to change.

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:20.070
Right?

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:22.980
Candidly, my biggest problem
I have right now is we

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:24.930
still get our fees in
from consultants who've

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:26.513
run Quick Sizer and
said, the customer

00:24:26.513 --> 00:24:27.940
want this three years from now.

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:28.320
I'm like, no.

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:29.730
They don't want this
three years from now.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:31.355
They want a minimal
cost operation that

00:24:31.355 --> 00:24:33.040
flexes with them as they go.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:34.980
They just didn't
know it was possible.

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:35.845
Right?

00:24:35.845 --> 00:24:37.470
Politely, the help
I need from you guys

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:39.360
as customers and
partners is I need

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:43.230
you to recognize the point
where you come in for a bid

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.180
to tell whoever's
doing the bid for you

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:47.060
that you want this flexibility.

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:48.600
Right?

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:50.640
We've built the foundation.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:53.220
Ultimately, by the way, you
guys will change the world.

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:54.120
Right?

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:56.393
We're just giving you
the ability to do it.

00:24:56.393 --> 00:24:58.060
This is the part where
now Nancy thinks,

00:24:58.060 --> 00:24:59.310
Paul sounds like he's insane.

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:00.647
But anyway.

00:25:00.647 --> 00:25:02.480
Reduced risk, let's
talk about reduced risk.

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:04.855
So when you get to operation
with Google Cloud, so great.

00:25:04.855 --> 00:25:07.330
We're cheaper and
everything else.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:08.630
Is it enough?

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:10.140
Well, again, I've
lived this life.

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:13.053
I've lived this
life myself where

00:25:13.053 --> 00:25:15.220
I'm old enough that I've
lived this life when I used

00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:18.250
to carry a pager, a real pager.

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:20.250
And I'd get paged and
say, the systems are down.

00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:23.160
And I actually, to tell the
truth, I started work for HP.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:26.583
I used to carry a dial in
terminal in the trunk of my car

00:25:26.583 --> 00:25:28.750
and have to find an acoustic
coupler and plug it in.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:30.543
That dates me for age.

00:25:30.543 --> 00:25:31.585
But I've lived this life.

00:25:31.585 --> 00:25:32.085
Right?

00:25:32.085 --> 00:25:36.070
None of us want to deal with,
hey, the system's not working.

00:25:36.070 --> 00:25:37.010
Right?

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:40.420
None of us want to deal with
the argument where the CFO says,

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:42.850
I know we have security
patches, but we've

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:45.600
got to get the
financial results out.

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:47.820
Why do you have to make
that compromise at all?

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:48.750
Right?

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:51.090
And so one of the
key things that we

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:53.632
work at in engineering is, OK,
if we're going to go entirely

00:25:53.632 --> 00:25:55.590
VM based, so if we're
going to go for a 100% VM

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:59.260
infrastructure, what
can we do better?

00:25:59.260 --> 00:25:59.760
Right?

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:01.740
There has to be
other capabilities.

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:03.973
And so I'll give
you one right now.

00:26:03.973 --> 00:26:05.640
So we have a tool
called live migration.

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:07.930
It applies to all of
our fleet, by the way.

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:10.740
But it really
works well for HANA

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.103
and for the SAP applications.

00:26:13.103 --> 00:26:14.520
So what live
migration does is you

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:16.120
are running your SAP
environment, application

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:17.662
servers, database
servers, everything

00:26:17.662 --> 00:26:19.020
else on a set of VMs.

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:20.010
Right?

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:23.130
And what happens right
now is, so let's take

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:24.120
Meltdown and Spectre.

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:26.890
One of the big security
patches comes along.

00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:28.890
Every other provider on
the planet, what happens

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:32.110
is you get an email on Patch
Tuesday that basically says,

00:26:32.110 --> 00:26:35.960
hey, there is a major
security vulnerability.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:38.720
All your machines have to
be patched within 72 hours.

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:40.287
So this is Tuesday.

00:26:40.287 --> 00:26:41.870
I'm not aware of a
single SAP customer

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:44.550
in the world who schedules
downtime Tuesday,

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:45.890
Wednesday, Thursday, or Friday.

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:46.490
Right?

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:48.470
But you don't have an option
because you have 72 hours,

00:26:48.470 --> 00:26:49.390
and that's what
you've got to do.

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:51.740
So you now have to take an
unscheduled outage in order

00:26:51.740 --> 00:26:52.500
to do the patch.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:54.083
Now what a lot of
other guys will say,

00:26:54.083 --> 00:26:55.670
well, we have
automatic patching.

00:26:55.670 --> 00:26:56.900
Yeah, you do.

00:26:56.900 --> 00:26:58.400
But you got to shut
the system down.

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:00.140
And then on reboot, it
automatically patches.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:01.310
And you're going
to take an outage.

00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:03.268
It doesn't count as an
outage for SLA purposes,

00:27:03.268 --> 00:27:05.300
but SAP ain't running.

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:07.730
So as far as you're
concerned, you're out.

00:27:07.730 --> 00:27:09.230
Right?

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:12.680
Why does that have to happen?

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:15.447
What we do with live migration
is something very different.

00:27:15.447 --> 00:27:17.530
At the point where we have
a security patch that's

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:21.160
ready to go, and because
we're entirely VM based,

00:27:21.160 --> 00:27:25.330
in the rack, the same rack
close to where your SAP

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:28.030
environment is
running, a second VM

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:29.710
will start up,
exactly the same size

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:32.340
and shape as the current
VM that you're running.

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:34.338
It will get prepatched
for security.

00:27:34.338 --> 00:27:35.880
And then a process
will run that will

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:38.443
sync the contents of
memory, CPU, and disk

00:27:38.443 --> 00:27:40.110
from the server you're
currently running

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:43.410
on to the new server that's
been patched while it continues

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:44.430
to run.

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:46.830
SAP continues to run completely.

00:27:46.830 --> 00:27:47.850
Systems aren't aware.

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.380
The users are
completely unaware.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.790
But it is being
replicated as it goes.

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:54.600
And at completion,
when we achieve sync,

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:58.680
the network will take a
millisecond pause, basically,

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:01.230
to allow the original server
you were running on to drop out,

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:03.000
the new server to take over.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:07.020
The data will start flowing
into it and with zero downtime

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:09.750
and an imperceptible
network pause,

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:14.470
you will be fully
patched with no downtime

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:19.480
across your entire SAP fleet,
across your entire fleet,

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:21.870
everything you run.

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:24.810
So do the math.

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:25.740
Right?

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:27.930
Every time you need to
do a security patch,

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:30.278
you don't need to
do a security patch.

00:28:30.278 --> 00:28:31.320
We handle it all for you.

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:32.940
And the really cool
part in all this

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:36.300
is, as much as I
say, everybody else

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:37.830
emails you on
Tuesday to tell you

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:39.385
you need to down your systems.

00:28:39.385 --> 00:28:41.010
And then you have to
go into chaos mode

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:43.150
to manage that and shut it down.

00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:45.150
We don't email you on Tuesday.

00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:47.370
If the flag is set
on the hypervisor,

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:52.300
we prepatch this before the
security alert goes out.

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:54.200
It happens completely
automatically.

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:56.200
You can turn it, off by
the way, if you want to.

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:57.190
Don't turn it all the way off.

00:28:57.190 --> 00:28:58.910
But you can actually
turn it off and say,

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:00.577
I would like to choose
the point in time

00:29:00.577 --> 00:29:03.250
when it happens over the
course of the next 72 hours.

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:05.907
Nobody does, but you can.

00:29:05.907 --> 00:29:07.990
If you don't believe this,
by the way, it's funny,

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:10.428
we had a customer
who said, well, this

00:29:10.428 --> 00:29:11.720
all sounds too good to be true.

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.178
And so we actually racked them
a system with primary and HA

00:29:14.178 --> 00:29:16.210
and Brussels one, Brussels
two, DR in London,

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:19.570
all running with an escort
system running on top of it.

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:20.650
And we said, have at it.

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:21.730
Do what you want.

00:29:21.730 --> 00:29:24.520
And the log file shows is
seven times in the first hour,

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:27.820
they pushed test live migration
while putting transactions in.

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:29.840
And an hour later, they
called us and said, hey,

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:31.360
this thing really works.

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:32.850
Yeah, we knew.

00:29:32.850 --> 00:29:34.460
But you had to prove
it to yourself.

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:35.220
And that's OK.

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:36.040
All right?

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:37.480
You can test it yourself.

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:40.210
You can provision a server,
deploy it, hit the button.

00:29:40.210 --> 00:29:42.350
It'll work.

00:29:42.350 --> 00:29:44.150
And again, just do that math.

00:29:44.150 --> 00:29:48.750
And that's politely I
will say one of the things

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:50.830
we've been working on.

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:53.990
Today is not the day to go
through all our box of tricks.

00:29:53.990 --> 00:29:56.930
But there's a lot more tricks
to come, all right, all of which

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:59.350
are focused on the
life of an SAP customer

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:01.850
and in getting rid of
all the things they

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:06.270
care about, right, sizing,
downtime, all of that stuff.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:07.698
We've built an
environment where I

00:30:07.698 --> 00:30:09.240
can deal with those
problems in a way

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:10.982
that no one's done it before.

00:30:10.982 --> 00:30:12.190
We talked about this already.

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:16.170
But again, $10 billion
manufacturer, it's

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:18.480
a million dollars per
hour in lost revenue.

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:20.040
Three hours,
basically, because it's

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:22.950
not just shutting down a single
server and rebooting it, right?

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:25.325
You have to shut down the
entire cluster, all the support

00:30:25.325 --> 00:30:27.090
in GRC, GTX, everything else.

00:30:27.090 --> 00:30:28.380
You have to patch them all.

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:30.120
You have to reboot them all
in the correct sequence.

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:31.170
It's a huge amount of times.

00:30:31.170 --> 00:30:32.962
It's three hours every
time they shut down.

00:30:32.962 --> 00:30:36.577
It's $3 million every time
there's an outage to $0.

00:30:36.577 --> 00:30:38.160
You don't have to
do too many of those

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:41.250
before, frankly, I've
built you in revenue way

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.740
more than my entire
contract costs.

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:44.580
Right?

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:46.470
The game's about to change.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:48.630
With that, I'll also
give a quick high five

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:50.820
to Intel Optane.

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:54.173
One of the bigger challenges
is that it varies depending

00:30:54.173 --> 00:30:55.590
on customer,
depending on chipset,

00:30:55.590 --> 00:31:00.720
but in general, per
terabyte of HANA on reboot,

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:02.220
it takes about an
hour per terabyte

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:04.990
to get back to full capacity
and operational run.

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:06.720
So if you're running
a 1 terabyte HANA,

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:07.680
that's a problem.

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:08.180
Right?

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:10.063
If you're running
a 4 terabyte HANA,

00:31:10.063 --> 00:31:12.480
you're looking at about three
hours potentially 45 minutes

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:13.380
to recovery.

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:17.160
You're looking at three hours
of either non-performance

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:18.630
or decreased performance.

00:31:18.630 --> 00:31:23.090
If you're running a big server,
12 or an 18 terabyte server,

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:26.590
that number becomes a
significant encumbrance to you.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:27.680
Right?

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:30.440
The reboot time becomes
the vastly biggest part

00:31:30.440 --> 00:31:31.140
of this problem.

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:33.140
And so one of things we've
been doing with Intel

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:35.210
is deploying Optane.

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:38.750
That, by the way, is that
7 terabyte box that's

00:31:38.750 --> 00:31:40.808
sitting on that chart
right now, which is it's

00:31:40.808 --> 00:31:42.600
the first release of
a cascade leak server.

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:44.332
It's the first release
of Optane server.

00:31:44.332 --> 00:31:46.040
And, by the way, it
was also the first VM

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:50.490
bigger than 6 terabytes
available in market.

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:52.148
And it's Optane based.

00:31:52.148 --> 00:31:53.690
So what happens with
the Optane chips

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:56.030
is they are non-volatile RAM.

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:58.490
So without getting into
some of the specifics

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:00.900
that I'm not allowed
to disclose right now,

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:03.560
a significant
percentage of your data

00:32:03.560 --> 00:32:05.960
is actually in non-volatile RAM.

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:08.210
And so when HANA shuts
down and restarts,

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:10.560
our hypervisor understands
where you were.

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:12.680
It reallocates you the
same non-volatile RAM

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:14.708
you had before that already
has your data in it.

00:32:14.708 --> 00:32:17.000
It's just going to populate
the little bit of data that

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:20.540
is the stuff that's
changing most frequently,

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:22.250
and you're fully
operational again.

00:32:22.250 --> 00:32:24.208
And so instead of taking
three hours of outage,

00:32:24.208 --> 00:32:26.230
you're taking 15
minutes of outage.

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:28.040
All right?

00:32:28.040 --> 00:32:30.230
So that's future as we walk
