that goes with change,
right, that has

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:31.710
nothing to do with technology.

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:35.110
User retraining, upgrades,
testing, there's [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:35.110 --> 00:40:37.740
So there's got to be something
else on the other side.

00:40:37.740 --> 00:40:39.240
What I think is
fascinating here is,

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:42.930
typically, we've relied on SAP,
we've relied on the partners,

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:46.230
we've relied on that stuff to
provide the something else.

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:48.570
What I couldn't find at
any of the other providers

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:50.160
was something else.

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:52.770
They we're quite happy to have
you Quick Size, sell you boxes,

00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:53.865
and be done.

00:40:53.865 --> 00:40:55.490
But I want to be more
than that to you.

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:57.100
I want to be a much
better partner.

00:40:57.100 --> 00:41:00.190
So inside that, we've been
working on the something else,

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:00.690
right?

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:04.680
So in the case of
BigQuery, BigQuery is now

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:06.605
pre-integrated with SAP, right?

00:41:06.605 --> 00:41:07.980
So you can start
Google BigQuery.

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:09.840
You can you put data
into Google BigQuery.

00:41:09.840 --> 00:41:11.370
You can join it to
SAP in real time

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:14.710
and do real-time analytics where
you have Google data and SAP

00:41:14.710 --> 00:41:15.210
data.

00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:17.370
So on its own, you're
going to say, yeah, cool.

00:41:17.370 --> 00:41:19.980
I mean, BigQuery is a
very, very cool tool.

00:41:19.980 --> 00:41:21.940
It's just crushing the
industry right now.

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:25.330
However, it's crushing it, I
should say, in the industry.

00:41:25.330 --> 00:41:27.970
However, it's an analytic tool.

00:41:27.970 --> 00:41:28.470
So what?

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:31.890
Once again, I will go back to,
yeah, but we have a set of data

00:41:31.890 --> 00:41:34.620
sets that we can put
into BigQuery for you.

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:35.430
Right?

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:38.350
We will give you an anonymized
data set of your advertising

00:41:38.350 --> 00:41:40.440
as it is running in
Google that you can then

00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:42.608
link back to the
geographic locations

00:41:42.608 --> 00:41:43.650
where you have inventory.

00:41:43.650 --> 00:41:47.540
And you can do far better
planning than you used to.

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:48.240
Right?

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:51.120
And that puts me in a place
where I'm not just a technology

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:55.933
provider for boxes for SAP, I'm
an innovation partner for you

00:41:55.933 --> 00:41:57.350
because I can deal
with analytics.

00:41:57.350 --> 00:41:58.310
I can deal with algorithm.

00:41:58.310 --> 00:41:59.768
I can apply machine
learning to it.

00:41:59.768 --> 00:42:02.000
There's a lot of stuff
I can now do, right?

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:04.550
So if the inventory systems
says there's four in the store,

00:42:04.550 --> 00:42:06.520
and it hasn't sold any
in the past eight hours,

00:42:06.520 --> 00:42:08.270
you can be pretty
certain all four of them

00:42:08.270 --> 00:42:09.900
have been stolen, right?

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:11.270
And you won't find that
until you go to the shelf.

00:42:11.270 --> 00:42:12.530
But you can actually
see it in the data.

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:13.940
You can run the calculation.

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:15.523
And the challenge
of Home Depot Canada

00:42:15.523 --> 00:42:18.740
is the supply lines are so long
for some of the their stores

00:42:18.740 --> 00:42:22.550
that by the time you
detect that, it could

00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:26.748
be a day from now
when you detect that,

00:42:26.748 --> 00:42:28.040
because there's huge data sets.

00:42:28.040 --> 00:42:29.180
It's all point of sale
data and everything else.

00:42:29.180 --> 00:42:31.702
So by the time you detect that,
it could be a day from now.

00:42:31.702 --> 00:42:33.410
And then it'll take
another day to get it

00:42:33.410 --> 00:42:34.460
on the next truck
that's going there.

00:42:34.460 --> 00:42:36.980
So you could be three days
out of stock on the item.

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:39.080
And the answer is
so a bunch of that

00:42:39.080 --> 00:42:43.610
as well as moving [INAUDIBLE]
and S/4 into Google Cloud

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:45.937
and running it live there,
they also stood up BigQuery.

00:42:45.937 --> 00:42:48.020
And inside BigQuery, they're
running that velocity

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:48.590
algorithm.

00:42:48.590 --> 00:42:49.910
And instead of
running it once a day,

00:42:49.910 --> 00:42:51.452
which is what they
were doing before,

00:42:51.452 --> 00:42:53.460
it runs every 15 minutes.

00:42:53.460 --> 00:42:55.520
So every 15 minutes, they
get the likely suspects

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:56.900
for what is not in store.

00:42:56.900 --> 00:42:58.700
And that way, instead
of it being 72 hours,

00:42:58.700 --> 00:43:00.740
you start to shrink
it down to 24 hours

00:43:00.740 --> 00:43:01.780
before it's replenished.

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:02.720
Right?

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:06.673
The world is full of
those opportunities.

00:43:06.673 --> 00:43:08.840
And again, we've tried to
build the best environment

00:43:08.840 --> 00:43:09.840
we can build to run SAP.

00:43:09.840 --> 00:43:12.048
But we've also integrated
it through to a whole bunch

00:43:12.048 --> 00:43:13.820
of other technology
where this isn't just,

00:43:13.820 --> 00:43:18.380
hey, what you had over there
is going to run over here.

00:43:18.380 --> 00:43:20.870
This is something much better.

00:43:20.870 --> 00:43:23.150
I said a year ago
when I started this,

00:43:23.150 --> 00:43:25.220
every conversation
started with, we just

00:43:25.220 --> 00:43:26.418
want to do a lift and shift.

00:43:26.418 --> 00:43:27.710
We just want to lift and shift.

00:43:27.710 --> 00:43:30.650
Frankly, I never understood
the concept of lift and shift.

00:43:30.650 --> 00:43:33.380
If it runs OK over
there, where is the value

00:43:33.380 --> 00:43:34.880
in running it over
here, especially

00:43:34.880 --> 00:43:36.530
in SAP, where we have
to do all the testing

00:43:36.530 --> 00:43:38.900
and all the rerouting of
interfaces and everything else,

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:39.500
right?

00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:41.480
There's got to be
something more.

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:42.350
Right?

00:43:42.350 --> 00:43:44.670
And so again, the something
more is better uptime.

00:43:44.670 --> 00:43:46.670
The something more is way
more flexible pricing.

00:43:46.670 --> 00:43:52.010
The something more is
vastly improved analytics,

00:43:52.010 --> 00:43:54.510
vastly improved
business outcomes.

00:43:54.510 --> 00:43:56.540
And that's why we
believe we're a better

00:43:56.540 --> 00:43:59.000
partner for the long term.

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:00.620
Last thing, however, is this.

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:03.690
But you guys know
my favorite thing?

00:44:03.690 --> 00:44:07.770
What you're looking at is
as a MiR lifter shifter.

00:44:07.770 --> 00:44:10.260
So basically, it's a
robot you dispatches off,

00:44:10.260 --> 00:44:12.570
where you say, hey, go over
there, pick up that pallet,

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:14.340
bring it back to me, drop it.

00:44:14.340 --> 00:44:16.890
So if I'm running pick
pack operations in a DC,

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:20.968
or if I'm doing
store shelf packing,

00:44:20.968 --> 00:44:22.260
basically, you dispatch it off.

00:44:22.260 --> 00:44:23.160
It grabs the stuff to you.

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:23.970
You stick it in boxes.

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:25.140
It does the walking for you.

00:44:25.140 --> 00:44:26.182
And you carry it through.

00:44:26.182 --> 00:44:28.220
Very cool.

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:32.450
One of the things we
look at in Google world

00:44:32.450 --> 00:44:36.040
is, OK, how can we again
extend and help our customers.

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:38.520
So what we had, we actually
showed this at TechEd Las Vegas

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:39.240
last year with SAP.

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:41.115
We had one of these
walking around the floor.

00:44:41.115 --> 00:44:43.410
We took one to TechEd
Barcelona as well.

00:44:43.410 --> 00:44:45.540
Pre-integrated to SAP.

00:44:45.540 --> 00:44:46.932
So robots are hard, right?

00:44:46.932 --> 00:44:48.390
By the time you
deploy them, you've

00:44:48.390 --> 00:44:49.320
got to wire up the networks.

00:44:49.320 --> 00:44:50.390
You've got to do the security.

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:51.932
You've got to do
all the programming.

00:44:51.932 --> 00:44:54.120
[INAUDIBLE] The MiR,
we don't make the MiR,

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:57.240
but we've embedded a Google
technology package in the MiR.

00:44:57.240 --> 00:44:59.550
You basically turn it on,
connect it to your Wi-Fi,

00:44:59.550 --> 00:45:02.670
and inside Google Cloud, you
register the serial number

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:03.940
and say, hey, I own this MiR.

00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:05.640
And it connects up
automatically to SAP.

00:45:05.640 --> 00:45:06.560
And it's that simple.

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:08.310
You really say, hey,
I need you to go over

00:45:08.310 --> 00:45:11.790
here, grab this shelving
unit, and bring it back to me.

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:13.515
And SAP communicates
to Google Cloud.

00:45:13.515 --> 00:45:15.140
Google Cloud communicates
to the robot.

00:45:15.140 --> 00:45:16.660
Robot goes grabs
it and come back.

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:19.200
Huge deal to start with, right?

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:20.382
That very cool?

00:45:20.382 --> 00:45:22.590
What I'll tell you, however,
is something even cooler

00:45:22.590 --> 00:45:23.400
than that.

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:25.290
So I've just missed
it on timing here.

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:28.800
But when the robot, I mean,
clearly, 17 times a minute

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:31.980
this doofus parks a forklift
in front of this robot.

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:35.707
When he parks the robot,
so it detects the forklift

00:45:35.707 --> 00:45:36.540
and walks around it.

00:45:36.540 --> 00:45:37.880
When it walks around
it, look at the front.

00:45:37.880 --> 00:45:39.755
There's two holes in
the front of this robot.

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:46.450
The two holes are cameras.

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:48.617
So obviously, it has to be
able to see the forklift.

00:45:48.617 --> 00:45:50.575
It has to be able to do
a bunch of other stuff.

00:45:50.575 --> 00:45:52.260
But let me tell you
what else it does.

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:55.890
As it walks around, it's able
to see the shelves, right?

00:45:55.890 --> 00:45:57.892
So when it loops
back around here,

00:45:57.892 --> 00:45:59.100
it's able to see the shelves.

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:00.065
It's able to see the boxes.

00:46:00.065 --> 00:46:02.315
And it's going to break them
down into a set of boxes.

00:46:06.880 --> 00:46:08.880
And on those boxes, it's
going to read the text.

00:46:08.880 --> 00:46:10.120
It's going to read
the serial numbers.

00:46:10.120 --> 00:46:11.453
It's going to read the barcodes.

00:46:11.453 --> 00:46:13.273
It's going to read labels.

00:46:13.273 --> 00:46:15.690
And it's going to call back
to SAP, and it's going to say,

00:46:15.690 --> 00:46:18.330
I am right now at
this point in store,

00:46:18.330 --> 00:46:20.430
in warehouse, in
wherever I am, and I

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:23.010
believe I have seven of these
things in this location.

00:46:23.010 --> 00:46:26.810
Is that what you believe
you have in inventory?

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:30.020
Continuous cyclical inventory.

00:46:30.020 --> 00:46:31.380
Drop it in.

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:32.310
Turn it on.

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:33.620
Connect it to the Wi-Fi.

00:46:33.620 --> 00:46:36.110
And register it.

00:46:36.110 --> 00:46:39.690
We are not just a provider
of boxes to host SAP.

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:40.757
Right?

00:46:40.757 --> 00:46:42.090
We are on a far different track.

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:44.050
We are going to
change this business.

00:46:44.050 --> 00:46:46.425
I'll give you one last thought
on the robots, by the way.

00:46:48.630 --> 00:46:52.780
Let's say you're in an industry
like oil and gas, or airplane,

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:56.910
whatever, where you have things
that don't come in boxes.

00:46:56.910 --> 00:46:58.630
What happens then?

00:46:58.630 --> 00:47:02.190
What we demonstrated at
both Barcelona and in Vegas

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:04.290
is we're also integrating
with a 3D scanner,

00:47:04.290 --> 00:47:07.020
not a 3D scanner like on
your desktop, great big giant

00:47:07.020 --> 00:47:08.470
thing on an arm.

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:11.047
And so let's say
you have oil pumps.

00:47:11.047 --> 00:47:13.380
When the oil pump comes in
the factory, you sit it down.

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:14.870
You roll the 3D
scanner around it.

00:47:14.870 --> 00:47:17.010
It will image the 3D scanner.

00:47:17.010 --> 00:47:19.575
The geometry patterns are
uploaded into the robots.

00:47:19.575 --> 00:47:21.930
As the robots go around and
they see an item on a shelf,

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:25.290
and they basically say, yeah,
you know what, I see that.

00:47:25.290 --> 00:47:26.670
It can't find a text on it.

00:47:26.670 --> 00:47:27.920
It can't find a serial number.

00:47:27.920 --> 00:47:28.890
It can't fit a barcode.

00:47:28.890 --> 00:47:30.210
So I went into
geometry analysis.

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:30.760
It's about this long.

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:32.490
It's about this [INAUDIBLE]
it has this many bolts

00:47:32.490 --> 00:47:33.480
on the end of it.

00:47:33.480 --> 00:47:35.020
What do you think it is?

00:47:35.020 --> 00:47:37.250
It's a pump 1152 rev three.

00:47:37.250 --> 00:47:37.750
Cool.

00:47:37.750 --> 00:47:40.260
There's a pump 1152 rev three
sitting at this location

00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:41.070
in the warehouse.

00:47:43.618 --> 00:47:45.410
I'm not aware of anyone
else that's talking

00:47:45.410 --> 00:47:47.140
about doing all of this stuff.

00:47:47.140 --> 00:47:49.370
And again, this is the
total package, right?

00:47:49.370 --> 00:47:53.762
Where we are reinventing
how it runs, making this

00:47:53.762 --> 00:47:54.470
massively easier.

00:47:54.470 --> 00:47:56.120
30 second provision globally.

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:58.550
Making it much
simpler to consume,

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:01.483
much cheaper to consume,
way higher uptime.

00:48:01.483 --> 00:48:02.900
And then you add
in the analytics,

00:48:02.900 --> 00:48:05.108
you add in the robotics,
there's a ton of other stuff

00:48:05.108 --> 00:48:05.698
we're doing.

00:48:05.698 --> 00:48:07.490
This is a very different
value proposition.

00:48:07.490 --> 00:48:11.070
Again, hopefully, I've
made that clear to you,

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:13.070
and you understand that
in today's conversation.

00:48:13.070 --> 00:48:15.290
I thank you for
coming one more time.

00:48:15.290 --> 00:48:20.000
And politely ask you
that this is, once again,

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:21.920
going to sound strange,
but we're not here

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:23.960
to be one more hosting
provider in the cloud.

00:48:23.960 --> 00:48:28.073
We're here to
change how SAP runs.

00:48:28.073 --> 00:48:29.990
I have a lot of very
talented people at Google

00:48:29.990 --> 00:48:32.190
who endeavor to
do that every day.

00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:34.010
But ultimately, it's
for you, but it's also

00:48:34.010 --> 00:48:35.060
with your assistance.

00:48:35.060 --> 00:48:36.743
If you appreciate
this, A, we need

00:48:36.743 --> 00:48:38.410
the phone call when
you want to try out,

00:48:38.410 --> 00:48:40.970
or, B, I need you to
understand this message.

00:48:40.970 --> 00:48:43.370
And I need you to start
setting that criteria

00:48:43.370 --> 00:48:45.882
when people come in and say,
hey, we need to go forwards.

00:48:45.882 --> 00:48:47.840
The answer is there's a
different way to do it.

00:48:47.840 --> 00:48:52.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

